# "McClean House and Barn, Gettysburg National Military Park, Pa." (Lenard E. Brown, Division of History, Office of Archeology and Historic Preservation, National Park Service, October 1, 1968): second reading, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading took two passages (the house of 1863 and the claim list). This reading adds the farm, the barn and house as the report describes them, the use of the buildings, the occupation of the fields before and during the battle, the sworn statements of the claim file, and what the report says of the shrubbery and the red barn.
# The report is a historian's study of 1968 that quotes the claim file of 1868 to 1883 (RG 92 of the National Archives and RG 2 of the Pennsylvania State Archives, named in its notes); the house and barn are described as they stood in 1968 unless a sentence says 1863. PDF pp. 22, 23, 26 and 27 were looked at as page images (digits read from the image).
@ NPS-MCCLEAN-HOUSE-BARN
%basis the claim file of Moses McClean (RG 92, National Archives; RG 2, Pennsylvania State Archives), regimental and officers' accounts, the Warren map of 1868-69, as the report names them
%images 22, 23, 26, 27

3 | general | The McClean farm lies north of Gettysburg on the Mummasburg road | - | 1863 (location) | stated | The report covers the house and barn on the farm of Moses McClean north of Gettysburg on the Mummasburg road | The report covers historical data on the house and barn on the farm of Moses McClean north of Gettysburg on the Mummasburg road.
12 | buildings | Page's Virginia battery stood at McLean's red barn, in a veteran's oration | STR-MCLEAN-BARN1 | 1863-07-01 | stated | The report cites an oration at the monument to the 90th Pennsylvania that locates Page's Virginia battery at McLean's red barn (the barn is called red) | #s11
18 | woodlots | Confederates massed under shelter of the McLean buildings and shrubbery north of the Mummasburg road (Col. Prey) | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The report quotes Col. Gilbert G. Prey of the 104th New York, who noted late on the first day of the battle that the Confederates had massed for attack under shelter of the McLean buildings and shrubbery north of the Mummasburg road | #s4
21 | hospitals | No evidence that the McClean buildings were hospitals or headquarters | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says nothing has been found to indicate whether the Confederates, who held the McClean farm for the rest of the battle after the Union retreat, used the buildings as hospitals or headquarters | Nothing has been found to indicate if they made use of the buildings as hospitals or headquarters | #s1
21 | buildings | Gordon's red barn where Ewell and Early had their headquarters: perhaps McClean's | STR-MCLEAN-BARN1 | 1863-07-01 (headquarters on the night of July 1, hedged) | hedged | The report quotes John B. Gordon, who rode at two in the morning to the red barn in which Generals Ewell and Early then had their headquarters, and says this could have been the red barn of Moses McClean | =This could have been the red barn of Moses {{MeClean.|McClean.}} | #s7
21 | damage | Moses McClean's claim of $1,138.35 for damage by both armies, paid by Pennsylvania in 1869 | - | 1863-07 (damage claimed; paid 1869) | stated | The report says Moses McClean filed a claim of $1,138.35 for damage to his real and personal property by both armies and that the State of Pennsylvania paid it in 1869 | =Moses {{MeClean|McClean}} filed a claim of $1,138.35 for damage to his real and personal property by both armies, and it was paid by the State of Pennsylvania in 1869.
22 | fields | Union cavalry camped in McClean's fields on June 30 and Confederates on June 26 | - | 1863-06 (before the battle) | stated | The report says the property suffered damage before the battle, the Union cavalry having encamped in McClean's fields on June 30 and the Confederates on June 26 | #s2
22 | buildings | The house and barn were apparently the only two buildings on the place and were not badly damaged | STR-MCLEAN-HOUSE1,STR-MCLEAN-BARN1 | 1863-07 (hedged: apparently) | hedged | The report says the house and barn, apparently the only two buildings on the place, had not been too badly damaged during the conflict | =The house and barn, apparently the only two buildings on the place,{{33|}} had not been too badly damaged during the conflict
22 | buildings | The red barn of the narratives stands on a north-south axis about 200 feet south of the house | STR-MCLEAN-BARN1 | 1863 (the barn of the narratives; position as stated in 1968) | stated | The report says the red barn that was mentioned so prominently in some narratives is located on a north-south axis about 200 feet south of the house | The red barn that was mentioned so prominently in some narratives is located on a north-south axis about 200 feet south of the house.
22 | buildings | Warren's map of 1868-69 shows only two wooden structures at the McClean farm | STR-MCLEAN-HOUSE1,STR-MCLEAN-BARN1 | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says Major-General Warren's map of the battlefield surveyed in 1868-69 shows only two wooden structures at the McClean farm | Major-General Warren's map of the battlefield at Gettysburg surveyed in 1868-69 shows only two wooden structures at the McClean farm.
22 | buildings | The McClean house as described in 1968: two stories, white brick and frame, facing south (the brick L is later than 1863) | STR-MCLEAN-HOUSE1 | ~1968 (house with the later brick L) | stated | The report describes the McClean house in its present state as a two story white brick and frame building facing south, and its note says the house of 1863 was narrower and the brick L was added later | The McClean house was a two story white brick and frame building facing south. | @23: The house in existence in 1863 was a narrow structure with 3 rooms on each floor.
23 | buildings | The original McClean house was about 25 by 20 feet, with additions made before and after the war | STR-MCLEAN-HOUSE1 | ~original house (date not given); additions before and after 1863 | stated | The report says the original house was about 25 x 20 feet and that subsequent additions were made before and after the Civil War | 25 x 20 feet and subsequent additions were made before and after the Civil War | @22: The original house was about
